\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Spatial distribution of zoning cases across the Austin municipal core ($N=7,074$).}}{10}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces 200ft parcel buffer geometries used for formal TCAD appraisal aggregation and generating spatial petition targets.}}{11}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Austin municipal zoning process. A valid protest petition representing 20\% of the area of lots immediately adjoining the proposed change triggers a supermajority requirement at City Council.}}{12}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Ex-ante predicted hotspot density vs.\ realized development events. To define hotspots against the highly skewed structural baseline, the map filters exclusively for the top decile (Top 10\%) of parcels with the highest predicted development hazard. Hexbins requiring a minimum point-density count are used to visually suppress isolated singleton sites and isolate genuine spatial clusters.}}{15}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Opposition risk precision-recall curves by model architecture: CatBoost, Random Forest, ElasticNet Logistic, and V-REx.}}{17}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Joint expected petition probability: geographic distribution of predicted development probability $\times $ ex-ante opposition risk across the 7,074 historical zoning cases. This composite measure is subject to compounding uncertainty from each constituent model stage.}}{18}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Global Model Calibration:} Parallel evaluation of calibration reliability and capture curves across the Stage A (Development Occurrence) and Stage C (Opposition Risk) modeling architectures. All probabilities are post-Isotonic out-of-fold predictions.}}{20}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Out-of-Distribution Model Degradation:} (a) Predictive performance decay across temporal offsets. (b) Predictive tracking across shifting regulatory council environments.}}{22}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Top 10 predictor groups after hierarchical correlation clustering (Spearman $|r| > 0.70$). Correlated features are merged into a single cluster and their importances are summed to correct for the collinear dilution effect. Red bars indicate multi-feature clusters; blue bars are stand-alone predictors.}}{22}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces SHAP beeswarm plot for the opposition model at the filing baseline. Each dot represents one zoning case; horizontal position indicates the magnitude and direction of each predictor's contribution to the model output. SHAP values use tree path-dependent conditional expectations, which handle correlated features more reliably than standard permutation importance. However, standard SHAP values can still shift credit between highly correlated predictors across different background samples; full pairwise interaction decomposition would require SHAP interaction values at substantially higher computational cost. The clustered importance in Figure~\ref {fig:feature_importance_clustered} provides the most robust correction for collinearity.}}{23}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Methodological causal inference graphs mapping the specific institutional mechanisms modeled in the thesis. \textbf {Panel A} illustrates the Sharp Regression Discontinuity (RD), isolating council delay ($Y$) via the statutory 20\% protest petition threshold running variable ($R$). \textbf {Panel B} illustrates the Difference-in-Differences Event Study, isolating council dissent ($Y$) arising from the intersection of the discrete HOME Phase 1 policy time shock ($t$) and parcel eligibility ($E$).}}{25}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Regression discontinuity at the 20\% valid petition threshold. Estimated discontinuity in council processing time.}}{26}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces HOME Phase 1 event-study coefficients relative to the implementation date.}}{27}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Geographic distribution of false positive predictions (right) against actual petition filings (left). False positives split 49.6\% west of I-35 and 50.4\% east, indicating that the model's over-predictions are geographically symmetric rather than concentrated in historically vulnerable neighborhoods.}}{30}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Stage A \& Stage C:} Grid search precision-recall AUC optimization heatmaps across maximum tree depth and learning rate parameters for the Development Occurrence and Opposition Risk models.}}{38}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Mean predicted probability of each argument frame by Council District. Each of the 10 Austin Council Districts is shown as a row; columns represent the five argument frames classified from hearing transcripts. Darker cells indicate districts where a given frame appears more frequently in hearing testimony. Values are normalized to the maximum cell value for visual comparison.}}{40}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Mean predicted argument-frame probability for cases with a valid protest petition filed (Opposed) versus cases without (Uncontested). Bars compare how frequently each thematic frame appears in hearing testimony across the two groups.}}{41}{figure.caption.31}%
